" (https://aimath.org/pastworkshops/graphstructureapprep.pdf)\nciteturn4view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 4; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://aimath.org/pastworkshops/graphstructureapprep.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 163\nL0@P0: Graph Theory: structural properties, labelings, and connections to applications\nL1@P0: organized by\nL2@P0: Katherine Benson, Christine Kelley, and Alison Marr\nL3@P0: Workshop Summary\nL4@P0: Introduction\nL5@P0: Our graph theory workshop was held July 22-26, 2024 at AIM on CalTech’s campus.\nL6@P0: We had 32 participants from across North America, Europe, and Asia. Our group included\nL7@P0: many mid-career faculty looking to restart their research program, some post-docs and junior\nL8@P0: faculty, and some senior faculty. Moreover, this AIM workshop supported the AWM Women\nL9@P0: in Graph Theory and Applications community and 75% of the participants were women.\nL10@P0: On the first day of the workshop, the group proposed possible research problems in three\nL11@P0: main areas: graph labeling/graph coloring, variations of zero forcing/power domination, and\nL12@P0: structural properties and connections. Over the course of the week, 9 groups were formed\nL13@P0: to look deeper into some of these questions. Most groups are continuing their collaboration\nL14@P0: after the workshop. There is a plan to have a special issue of The American Mathematical\nL15@P0: Monthly devoted to the work produced from our workshop. We also plan to run a special\nL16@P0: session at a future conference to highlight results stemming from this workshop.\nL17@P0: The week can be summarized by this word cloud generated on the last day of the\nL18@P0: workshop:\nL19@P0: We want to thank AIM for providing the space and resources for us to gather and\nL20@P0: collaborate.\nL21@P0: Group Reports\nL22@P0: (1) Edge Total Magic Labeling\nL23@P0: An edge magic total (EMT) labeling of a graph G = (V, E) is a bijection from the\nL24@P0: set of vertices and edges to the set of integers defined by λ : V ∪ E → {1, 2, . . . , |V |+\nL25@P0: |E|} so that for every edge xy ∈ E, its weight defined by w(xy) = λ(x) + λ(y) +\nL26@P0: λ(xy) = k, for some integer k. An EMT labeling is said to be a super EMT labeling\nL27@P0: if the vertices are labeled with the smallest possible integers 1, 2, . . . , |V |. During\nL28@P0: the week at AIM workshop, we studied the open problem of EMT labeling on the\nL29@P0: disjoint union of cycles. Note that for 2-regular graphs, a graph has an edge magic\nL30@P0: total labeling if and only if it has a vertex magic labeling. Therefore, we studied the\nL31@P0: paper “Vertex magic total labelings of 2-regular graphs” by Cichacz, Froncek, and\nL32@P0: Singgih, where they conjectured that for even values of n, 2Cn has a vertex magic\nL33@P0: total labeling with k = 5n + 2 where k is the magic constant. We understood their\nL34@P0: constructions and how they generalized their labeling to a larger graph. We also\nL35@P0: studied the paper “A magical approach to some labeling conjectures” by Figuero\u0002Centeno et. al. We narrowed down the open problems to 2Cn where n = 2p (p > 7\nL36@P0-1: 12\nL37@P1: prime), n = 2q, q ≥ 4 and n odd, n ≡ 3, 9 (mod 12). In addition, we made some\nL38@P1: useful observations.\nL39@P1: (2) Proportional Zero Forcing\nL40@P1: Our group introduced a new variant of the zero forcing coloring game on graphs.\nL41@P1: Let G = (V, E) be a graph and assume that each vertex is colored either blue or\nL42@P1: white, but not both. In standard zero forcing, we start with an initial subset of blue\nL43@P1: vertices and obey the following color change rule: if at time t, v is blue and v has\nL44@P1: exactly one white neighbor x, then the color of x changes to blue. We say F ⊆ V is\nL45@P1: a zero forcing set of G if the vertices in F are initially assigned the color blue, and\nL46@P1: after repeated application of the color change rule, all vertices in G become blue.\nL47@P1: The zero forcing number Z(G) of G is the minimum cardinality of a zero forcing set.\nL48@P1: For example, Z(Kn) = n − 1 for the complete graph Kn since any vertex has at\nL49@P1: least 2 white neighbors unless all but one vertices are initially colored blue.\nL50@P1: In proportional zero forcing, we assume that the ability of a blue vertex to turn\nL51@P1: another vertex blue is no longer binary but instead determined by a quantity that\nL52@P1: degrades through the transmission process. Let G = (V, E) be a graph and assume\nL53@P1: that each vertex is colored either blue or white, but not both, and each vertex y has\nL54@P1: an assigned weight, given by w(y). The weight function and coloring will change\nL55@P1: according to the proportional color change rule: at time t, for a white vertex x with\nL56@P1: any blue neighbor v such that x is the only white neighbor of v, each such v adds\nL57@P1: α · w(v) to w(x), and if the total new weight of x is at least β, then the color of\nL58@P1: x changes to blue. We assume that the effective transmission proportion α ∈ [0, 1]\nL59@P1: and the effective transmission threshold β ∈ [0, 1] are constant for all transmissions\nL60@P1: and fixed in time. The proportional zero forcing number of G for α, β, denoted by\nL61@P1: Zα,β(G), is the minimum size of an initial blue set F such that repeated application\nL62@P1: of the proportional color change rule results in all vertices of G being blue.\nL63@P1: Returning to our earlier example, we note that Zα,β(Kn) = n − 1 if (n − 1)α ≥ β and Zα,β(Kn) =\nL64@P1: (3) g(e) = (\nL65@P1: f(e) if f(e) ̸= j\nL66@P1: i if f(e) = j\nL67@P1: for all e ∈ E(G).\nL68@P1: If this implies there exists u ̸= v such that S(u) ̸= S(v) for all i ̸= j, then f is a\nL69@P1: minimal VDEC.\nL70@P1: For graph G, denote the minimum k such that G admits a VDEC with k colors as\nL71@P1: χ\nL72@P1: ′\nL73@P1: S\nL74@P1: (G). This chromatic VDEC number has been studied before. We defined the achromatic\nL75@P1: VDEC number to be the maximum k such that G admits a minimal VDEC with k colors\nL76@P1: as Ψ′\nL77@P1: S\nL78@P1: (G). We worked on determining Ψ′\nL79@P1: S\nL80@P1: (G) for several classes of graphs. Furthermore,\nL81@P1: we realized we could modify the definition to create related VDEC measures. For example,\nL82@P1: rather than using the edge coloring to create unique sets of the incident edges for each vertex,\nL83@P1: we could define S(v) to be the multi-set of colors incident to vertex v where we still want\nL84@P1: each S(v) to be unique. The minimum k such that G admits a multi-set VDEC with k colors\nL85@P1: is denoted as χ\nL86@P1: ′\nL87@P1: MS(G) and the maximum k such that G admits a minimal multi-set VDEC\nL88@P1: with k colors is Ψ′\nL89@P1: MS(G). We then began to consider ways we could leverage other known\nL90@P1: graphical results, such as the existence of a 1-factor or not, to help determine Ψ′\nL91@P1: MS(G).\nL92@P1-2: Non-trivial square graphs3\nL93@P2: Motivated by recent work of Garg, Raymond, and Redlich, this group explored which\nL94@P2: graphs and families of graphs admit a non-trivial square root. This line of research has\nL95@P2: ties to Sidorenko’s conjecture, in particular, whether or not the Cauchy-Schwarz sum-of\u0002squares approach is a feasible proof technique for some specific bipartite graph families. Past\nL96@P2: work has classified a number of bipartite graph families for which Sidorenko’s conjecture is\nL97@P2: known. During the workshop, group participants familiarized themselves with the problem,\nL98@P2: its motivation, and known results. The group then explored additional graph families and\nL99@P2: proved necessary and sufficient conditions for some families of graphs to admit non-trivial\nL100@P2: squares.\nL101@P2: Graph homomorphisms\nL102@P2: Graph homomorphisms—functions from between the vertex sets of graphs that preserve\nL103@P2: adjacency — provide one lens through which graph labelling can be viewed. We consider an\nL104@P2: extremal enumerative question in the field of graph homomorphisms: for each graph H and\nL105@P2: integer n, which tree on n vertices admits the fewest homomorphisms to H? In other words,\nL106@P2: which tree admits the fewest labelings, where the vertices of H are the labels, and adjacent\nL107@P2: vertices in the tree must be given labels that are adjacent in H? When “fewest” above is\nL108@P2: replaced by “least”, the answer is well known, but there is a lot still to be discovered in the\nL109@P2: minimization version of the problem.\nL110@P2: We studied a specific construction in the literature, and sketched how to generalize\nL111@P2: it from one specific instance of the problem (where it shows that for n = 7 the problem\nL112@P2: will not admit a nice uniform answer) to an infinite family of instances. We obtained a\nL113@P2: new infinite class of graphs for which the minimizing tree (for every n) is the path. We\nL114@P2: discussed a number of concrete questions and conjectures around the problem, perhaps the\nL115@P2: most appealing of which is the conjecture that for every H, the path is the minimizing tree\nL116@P2: for all sufficiently large n.\nL117@P2: The group has started meeting regularly via zoom to continue this work.\nL118@P2: Neighborhood Balanced Colorings:\nL119@P2: A neighborhood balanced coloring, introduced by Freyberg and Marr in the paper\nL120@P2: “Neigborhood Balanced Colorings of Graphs” in Graphs and Combinatorics, is a vertex col\u0002oring of a simple graph G by two colors such that every vertex in G has an equal number\nL121@P2: of neighbors of each color. During the week several variations and generalizations of neigh\u0002borhood balanced colorings were proposed and studied. One variation included extending\nL122@P2: neighborhood balanced colorings to more than two colors, particularly looking at 3-colorings\nL123@P2: on circulant graphs. Another variation of the problem that became the focus of much of the\nL124@P2: work was closed neighborhood balanced colorings. A closed neighborhood balanced coloring\nL125@P2: (CNBC) of a graph is a red-blue coloring of its vertices such that the closed neighborhood of\nL126@P2: every vertex contains an equal number of red and blue vertices. If a graph G admits such a\nL127@P2: coloring, we say G is a CNBC graph. The work on CNBC graphs involved establishing the\nL128@P2: necessary notation, definitions, and preliminary results, as well as proving results for specific\nL129@P2: families of graphs, trees, and graph operations.\nL130@P2: Radio Graceful Graphs:\nL131@P2: For a simple connected graph G, a labeling f : V (G) → Z+ is a radio labeling of G if it\nL132@P2: satisfies |f(u) − f(v)| ≥ diam(G) + 1 − d(u, v) for all distinct vertices u, v in V (G). A radio\nL133@P2: labeling f of a graph G is a consecutive radio labeling if f(V (G)) = {1, 2, . . . , |V (G)|}. A\nL134@P2-3: graph for which a consecutive radio labeling exists is a radio graceful graph.4\nL135@P3: In this workshop, we worked to determine graphs which are radio graceful. In partic\u0002ular, this group focused on looking at Cartesian products of some graphs. In the process\nL136@P3: of looking at these Cartesian products, we introduced the concept of circular radio graceful\nL137@P3: graphs. In addition, we worked to develop constructions of radio graceful graphs that are\nL138@P3: diameter 2. The group continues to meet to work on each of these projects connected to\nL139@P3: radio graceful graphs.\nL140@P3: Induced Subgraphs\nL141@P3: Let d < k < n2 and let G be a bipartite graph with partite sets X and Y where the\nL142@P3: vertices in X correspond to all possible d subsets of [n] and the vertices in Y correspond to\nL143@P3: all possible k subsets of [n]. For x ∈ X and y ∈ Y , the edge xy ∈ E(G) if l(x) ⊆ l(y) where\nL144@P3: l(x) and l(y) are the subsets of [n] that correspond to x and y, respectively. Our group\nL145@P3: met to discuss the possible induced subgraphs of this graph G. Our group met just one day\nL146@P3: during the workshop and explored the answer to this question for small values of k, d, and\nL147@P3: n.\nL148@P3: Distance Antimagic\nL149@P3: A graph G is called distance magic if there exists a bijection f : V (G) → {1, 2, . . . , v}\nL150@P3: such that at any vertex x, the weight of x, ω(x) = P\nL151@P3: y∈N(x)\nL152@P3: f(y) is constant, where N(x)\nL153@P3: is the open neighbourhood of x, i.e., the set of vertices adjacent to x. A graph G is said\nL154@P3: to be distance antimagic if there is a bijection f : V (G) → {1, 2, . . . , v} such that for every\nL155@P3: pair of distinct vertices x and y applies w(x) ̸= w(y). In this case, the bijection f is called\nL156@P3: antimagic labeling of G.\nL157@P3: Our group met to investigate when the Cartesian product of two graphs is distance\nL158@P3: antimagic for different classes of component graphs. The component graphs considered\nL159@P3: included complete graphs, complete bipartite graphs, wheel graphs, cycle graphs, and path\nL160@P3: graphs. We also examined the lexicographic product of different classes of graphs. Several\nL161@P3: of our results start with at least one graph that is distance magic, and exploit that labeling\nL162@P3: to obtain an antimagic labeling of the corresponding product graph.\n"